>>             util.sign = 1;
>>           }
>>           else
>>           {
>>             z = &z_minus;
>>             rho = &rho_minus;
>>             util.sign = -1;
>>           }
>>
>>           this->_z.copy_base(*z);
>>
>>           int n_valid_subtree = build_tree(_depth, *rho, 0, z_propose, util);
>>
>>           *z = static_cast<ps_point>(this->_z);
>>
>> You don't need the init to 0 here, as they'll get set whichever
>> way you go through the branch.
>
> (Right, but ps_point is also used in many other files.  Rather safe than sorry.)

The point here is that you're safe not setting it at all, because
it's set in one of the branches of the if/then.  You have to be able to rely
on if/then doing the right thing!

My larger point was that it's NOT safe to just assume a "reasonable" default.
Quite the opposite --- it might cover up some potential problem.
It's much safer to init with NaN, so you know if you fail to override
the init you'll have a problem.

>> The above could be recoded as follows using references (and less
>> vertical space) as:
>>
>>           ps_point& z = &z_plus;
>>           Eigen::VectorXd& rho = &rho_plus
>>           if (this->_rand_uniform() > 0.5) {
>>             util.sign = 1;
>>           } else {
>>             z = &z_minus;
>>             rho = &rho_minus;
>>             util.sign = -1;
>>           }
>>           this->_z = z;
>>           int n_valid_subtree = build_tree(_depth, rho, 0, z_propose, util);
>>           z = &(this->z_);
>
> Does the second call work?  i.e. once z has been created as a reference, does z = &z_minus change the reference

Yes, it changes the reference.

> or call operator equals on z_plus?  Setting z_plus = z_minus would obviously break the algorithm.

Just setting z_plus = z_minus would employ the copy constructor
to set whatever z_plus refers to to hold copy of z_minus.

> I tried to get everything to work with references but failed, hence the pointers.
>
>> And there's still something fishy about having to use "this->" that
>> I don't understand.
>
> My best understanding is that there's some collision with the templates and the extension of the base class so you have to use this-> to reference any protected members of the base class.

I'll try to sort this out.  All the this's are very
distracting to me.

- Bob

